Release the emulator state that each opened track allocates.

usf_clear() mallocs an 8 MB save-state buffer and usf_startup() maps about
17 MB of emulated N64 memory, but the decoder never called usf_shutdown(), so
every opened track leaked roughly 24 MB. On PS5 a few tracks exhausted memory
and lazyusf crashed in memset(-1, ...) (see 0002). ReadTag() also allocated a
state into the playing instance's ctx.state without freeing it, and the Init()
failure path used delete instead of delete[] and left a dangling pointer for
the destructor to free again. Init() now also fails when the first render
fails instead of reporting a bogus sample rate.

Upstream: generic bug, suitable for xbmc/audiodecoder.usf.

--- a/src/USFCodec.h
+++ b/src/USFCodec.h
@@ -57,6 +57,7 @@
   bool ReadTag(const std::string& filename, kodi::addon::AudioDecoderInfoTag& tag) override;
 
 private:
+  void ReleaseState();
   bool CheckEndReached(uint8_t* buffer, int size);
   USFContext ctx;
   bool m_firstRoundDone = false;
--- a/src/USFCodec.cpp
+++ b/src/USFCodec.cpp
@@ -181,7 +181,18 @@
 
 CUSFCodec::~CUSFCodec()
 {
+  ReleaseState();
+}
+
+void CUSFCodec::ReleaseState()
+{
+  if (!ctx.state)
+    return;
+  // usf_clear() already allocates the save-state buffer; usf_shutdown()
+  // releases it along with the ROM pages and emulated RDRAM.
+  usf_shutdown(ctx.state);
   delete[] ctx.state;
+  ctx.state = nullptr;
 }
 
